# Color Hot Path Rules

The capture -> splitter -> device path runs 30-60 times per second. Treat it like a
real-time loop.

## No per-frame allocations

- Do NOT allocate `Mat`s, arrays, or LINQ chains (`ToArray`, `Reverse`, `Skip`) inside
  per-frame code. Reuse preallocated buffers (see `FrameSplitter`'s persistent
  `_resizeMat`/`_gammaMat`/`_warpMat`, ROI cache, and double-buffered color arrays).
- Color output arrays are double-buffered and reused: consumers must copy what they
  need synchronously inside the `ColorSendEventAsync` handler, never hold references
  across frames.

## FrameSplitter.Update contract

- The CALLER owns the input `Mat` and disposes it; `Update` never disposes inputs.
- `Update` drops overlapping frames (`IsBusy`) - latest frame always wins; never queue
  frames behind an in-flight update. Always `await Update(frame)` before disposing the frame.
- Capture callbacks (BASS, MMAL, DreamScreen) must not block: check `Splitter.IsBusy`
  and drop, or hand off to a worker (see `AudioStream.RenderLoop`).

## LED / power conventions

- `LedData.Brightness` and device brightness in the UI are 0-100 percent. Convert with
  `LedAgent.ToByteBrightness` when talking to the driver (0-255).
- ABL (auto brightness limiting) lives in `LedAgent.VoltAdjust`: per-LED draw is summed,
  compared against `AblVolts * AblAmps` (with 10% headroom), and brightness is scaled
  through an EMA (fast attack, slow release). Don't reintroduce per-frame snapping.
- Always pass explicit GPIO pins (`(Pin)LedData.GpioNumber`) to `Settings.AddController`;
  never rely on the wrapper's defaults for dual-strip setups.
